<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Architecture fitness tests (docs/architecture/solution-layout.md 1, testing-strategy.md 5).

    No database, no container, fast: verify.sh runs these as their own stage (7) so a layering
    breach is reported as a layering breach rather than buried in a module's test output. Until
    B-11 implements stage 7 they run inside stage 6, which is why every test class here carries
    [Trait("Category", "Architecture")] - stage 7 will select on it, and stage 6's
    Category!=Integration&Category!=Ui filter already includes it.

    Read README.md in this folder first: it lists every rule, what its mechanism actually
    inspects, and which rules are inert until the types they govern exist.

    The rules read compiled assemblies with System.Reflection.Metadata (in-box) rather than
    reflection or ArchUnitNET. ADR-0020 chose ArchUnitNET for architecture tests and noted that
    "source-level rules are Roslyn tests, because reflection cannot see them"; docs/reviews/B-03.md
    m-1 is the proof - a rule reflecting over member signatures cannot see a `double` local or a
    `(double)` cast, which is exactly the violation solution-layout.md 6 row B-04 requires this
    rule set to catch. IL is where that violation is visible. README.md 5 records the deviation.

    The EntityFrameworkCore reference and the ASP.NET framework reference exist for ONE reason:
    the deliberately-violating fixtures in Fixtures/ derive from the real DbContext, use the real
    IHttpContextAccessor, and implement the real IHostedService / derive from the real
    BackgroundService, so the tenancy rules are proven to fire against the framework types they
    name rather than against look-alikes. FixtureFidelityTests asserts exactly that, so this
    comment is checked rather than trusted. No rule and no production code depends on either.

    The Npgsql provider reference and the Aurora.Platform.Tenancy.Contracts project reference
    exist for the migration rules (MIG1, MIG2): ADR-0007 7.2 says the safety test "generates each
    migration's SQL", and the SQL that runs against a tenant is the SQL Npgsql's own
    IMigrationsSqlGenerator emits from a migration's UpOperations - not the C# that produced them
    and not only the raw strings passed to migrationBuilder.Sql(). Generating it means loading each
    production migration assembly and running Up(); MigrationSafety/MigrationPopulation.cs says how and
    what that cannot see. The Contracts reference is the real [MigrationSafety] attribute, so the
    fixture migrations carry the type production migrations carry rather than a look-alike.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Aurora.Architecture.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" />
    <PackageReference Include="Shouldly" />
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" />
    <PackageReference Include="Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\platform\Aurora.Platform.Tenancy.Contracts\Aurora.Platform.Tenancy.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
